{"id":"h125","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A boy who is holding a suitcase stands in an elevator. The elevator has an acceleration A^\\ding{217}, which may be directed upward (A^\\ding{217}. positive) or downward (A^\\ding{217} negative). What force does the suitcase exert on the boy's hand?\nIs the correct answer \"m(A/g)\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"m(A/g) is not the correct force on the boy's hand.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A/g is dimensionless, so m(A/g) has units of mass rather than force.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A force must have units of newtons, so an expression with units of mass cannot be right.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"By Newton's second law, the hand's upward force on the suitcase satisfies T - mg = mA, giving T = m(g + A).","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"By Newton's third law, the suitcase pulls down on the hand with magnitude m(g + A).","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"When A is negative the magnitude is m(g - |A|), which is still a force.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The correct result depends on g + A, not on the ratio A/g.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"m(A/g) lacks a gravity term, giving zero force at rest when it should be mg.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The correct answer is m(g + A), directed downward on the hand.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,